<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu Mahasabha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Akhil Bharatiya Hindu Mahasabha, Sarvadeshik Hindu Sabha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Mahasabha is a far-right <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> political party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1915 at the Kumbh Mela in Haridwar as an umbrella body of regional Hindu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabhas</a>. Under Madan Mohan Malaviya it worked mainly as a pressure group for orthodox Hindus within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. In the 1930s it emerged as a distinct party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a>, a fierce opponent of the secular nationalism of the Congress. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it supported the British war effort and briefly formed coalition governments with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse">Nathuram Godse</a>, who assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, was a member, and after independence the party was soon eclipsed by the Bharatiya Jana Sangh.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Local forerunners of the Hindu Mahasabha emerged in disputes after the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1905. The founding of the All India Muslim League in 1906 and the separate Muslim electorates created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-councils-act-1909">Morley-Minto reforms</a> of 1909 spurred Hindu leaders to organise. In 1909 Lal Chand and U. N. Mukerji founded the Punjab Hindu Sabha, which held the Punjab Provincial Hindu Conference on 21 and 22 October 1909, criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> for failing to defend Hindu interests. Similar Hindu Sabhas arose in the United Provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, Bengal and Bombay Presidency.</p><p>In April 1915 the Sarvadeshak Hindu Sabha was formed as an umbrella body at the Kumbh Mela in Haridwar, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and Swami Shraddhanand present and supportive. In April 1921 it took its present name, modelled on the Congress, and amended its constitution to remove a clause about loyalty to the British.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early leaders included Madan Mohan Malaviya, founder of Benaras Hindu University, and Lala Lajpat Rai. Under Malaviya the Mahasabha campaigned for Hindu political unity, for Hindu education and economic development, and for the conversion of Muslims to Hinduism. It did not support the independence movement unconditionally but joined it on its own terms: it boycotted the Simon Commission, rejected the Nehru Report as conceding too much to Muslims, and helped secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a>. It abstained from the Civil Disobedience Movement of 1930. After the Congress won the 1937 provincial elections, the Mahasabha joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> and other parties in coalition governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, the North West Frontier Province and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> after the Congress ministries resigned in 1939.</p><p>In the 1930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a> led it as a distinct party and developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a>. It opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement">Quit India Movement</a>, and under Savarkar its Hindu Militarisation Boards recruited for the British Indian armed forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Savarkar also defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> as citadels of Hindu power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 30 January 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse">Nathuram Godse</a> assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. Godse and fellow conspirators were identified as prominent members of the Mahasabha, and police also arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Savarkar</a>, who was suspected of masterminding the plot but was released for lack of evidence. The Kapur Commission found that the facts were destructive of any theory other than a conspiracy to murder. The Mahasabha's fortunes declined after independence, and it was soon eclipsed by the Bharatiya Jana Sangh. The party identifies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a Hindu Rashtra, or Hindu nation, and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system and untouchability, holding all-caste dinners at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and Kanpur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hindu Mahasabha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar insisted that freedom must end caste oppression; the <b>Hindu Mahasabha</b> and the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh, founded in 1925, promoted Hindu nationalism; and most princes, who met in the Chamber of Princes from 1921, wished to keep their thrones, while popular movements in their states demanded…</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Savarkar, an atheist, opposed the caste system: under him the <b>Hindu Mahasabha</b> held all-caste dinners in Nagpur and Kanpur, and he gave parents money so that their children of the so-called lower castes would attend school.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
